  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 PM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nEast Idaho is under the influence of a shortwave passing to the\nnorth. Nose of moisture rides the shortwave into the central\nmountains this afternoon, then spreads east to the Divide and\neastern highlands overnight. Warm nose raises snow levels,\nkeeping accumulating snow above 6500 ft. Initial round of\nmoisture is light. Second shortwave brings stronger surge of\nmoisture late Friday through Saturday morning, with continued\nwarm snow levels. West to northwest flow favors orographic\nprecipitation mainly across the central mountains and eastern\nhighlands, but we could see light precipitation across the rest\nof the forecast area late Friday night. Have nudged QPF amounts\nupwards slightly especially where higher confidence in\norographic influence exists, and low-end probability amounts have \nincreased. This has resulted in a slight 1bump in snow totals, \nespecially across higher elevations above pass level.\nTotal accumulations still ranging right around 6\" for Galena\nSummit through Saturday morning. Further east, portions of the\nBig Holes and the higher elevations bordering Yellowstone\nranging 6-10\". This is still borderline for travel impacts, so\nwill continue to hold off on headlines. Cold front and shortwave\naxis pass through early Saturday, pushing precipitation east of\nthe region through the day. East Idaho under the influence of\nprogressive ridge shifting across the region through Sunday. The\nnext system arrives Sunday night through Monday for another\nround of higher elevation snow. Following rather quickly, models\nstill favor development of split flow with the main low shifting\ninland well south of Idaho. Ensemble clusters agree on the\ndevelopment of the split, but are slightly off on depth and\ntiming. NBM carries higher PoPs Tuesday into Tuesday night as\nthe split develops, then pulls the precipitation south Wednesday\nand beyond. Despite the high chances, QPF amounts remain light\nat this time.",
